      {
        "slide": 2,
        "role": "evidence",
        "subject": "the return plate in plan as a TXBENCH relief. It has 20 columns of bores, starting under a slate machined chamfer along its top edge with a warm caliche strip above. Five full rows and one extra plug of warm brass hex plugs sit at the top, and countersunk threaded open bores below each show a lit crescent wall around a black core, fading under the footer toward a final row of 14.",
        "visual_consequence": "With the headline covered, a warm block of sealed hexes takes about a third of a heavy iron plate and the rest is dark open holes, so the share reads as an area. The 101 can be counted as five rows of 20 plus one.",
        "weakness": "Nothing in this change breaks the frame. The chamfer still reads as a cool slate band about 20 design px deep, and the new grain and arris barely show at native size. The stronger footer wash puts the last two rows, including the final row of 14, near the floor of visibility, so at feed size the 334 can no longer be checked from the image. The share and the 101 still read and the headline states both numbers.",
        "repair": "Optional, for the panel. End the footer wash just above the final row, or hold it at 0.3 below design y 1270, so the row of 14 stays visible at 432 px.",
